SUPERSEDED 2026-09-11 -- THE DEFECT THIS FILE ISOLATES IS FIXED.

Read this header before anything below it. The measurements below are a
correct record of the state on 2026-09-10 and the isolation they describe is
what LED to the fix, but the "MFMV ON" column no longer describes the port.

WHAT THE DEFECT WAS. `sb64_sq_no4xn_geom` -- C's `ctx->sb64_sq_no4xn_geom`,
which selects the SIMPLIFIED MFMV block walk -- was set from `sb_size == 64`
ALONE. That is true at every preset this port ships, so the simplified walk ran
even where rectangular blocks exist, and it uses `n4_w` for BOTH the row and
the column extent: on a 16x32 it scans four rows instead of eight and the lower
half never contributes a temporal candidate. NEARESTMV and NEARMV derive their
MV from that stack, so encoder and decoder picked different motion vectors for
the same block -- exactly the signature recorded below. The condition now also
requires `!nsq_geom_enabled` and `disallow_4x4`, as C's does.

RE-MEASURED 2026-09-11, same harness, MFMV **ON**, 256x256 p6, 8 frames:
ALL 18 cells (six clips x qp {20,40,55}) reconstruct byte-identically to
`aomdec` for EVERY frame, including the vidyo1 qp20 cell this file names as a
separate residual bug. There is no remaining cell in this matrix where the
port's own reconstruction differs from a decoder's.

That result is now GATED by `tools/video_selfcheck_gate.sh`, so it cannot
regress silently. The gate has teeth: reverting the `sb64_sq_no4xn_geom`
condition to `sb_size == 64` alone turns 11 of its 18 cells into RECON MISMATCH
or UNDECODABLE -- measured 2026-09-11, which is also the evidence that the
condition is what fixed it.

`SVTAV1_MFMV_OFF` is kept as the isolation switch; it is still not a feature
flag, because C signals `use_ref_frame_mvs = 1`.

================================================================
ORIGINAL 2026-09-10 RECORD FOLLOWS
================================================================

The remaining multi-frame video defect is the TEMPORAL MOTION-VECTOR FIELD
— isolated 2026-09-10

`SVTAV1_MFMV_OFF` (new, default-off, measurement only) builds the ref-MV stack
from spatial candidates ONLY and signals `use_ref_frame_mvs = 0` to match, so
encoder and decoder agree. Both halves must move together: gating only the
header desynchronises from frame 0, because the port builds the field
unconditionally. That mistake was made first, and it produced 0-of-8 decoded
streams which briefly looked like evidence AGAINST the hypothesis. It was not
evidence of anything.

MEASURED, encoder recon (SVTAV1_FINAL_RECON) vs aomdec, 256x256 p6, 8 frames:

  qp40           MFMV ON                       MFMV OFF
  fourpeople     ALL 8 CLEAN                   ALL 8 CLEAN
  kristenandsara ALL 8 CLEAN                   ALL 8 CLEAN
  johnny         ALL 8 CLEAN                   ALL 8 CLEAN
  vidyo3         8/8 decoded, drift f3         ALL 8 CLEAN
  vidyo1         6/8 decoded, drift f4         ALL 8 CLEAN
  vidyo4         4/8 decoded                   ALL 8 CLEAN

  qp20           vidyo3 4/8 -> ALL 8 CLEAN, johnny drift f2 -> ALL 8 CLEAN,
                 vidyo4 2/8 -> ALL 8 CLEAN, vidyo1 2/8 -> STILL FAILS at f2
  qp55           all four clips ALL 8 CLEAN both ways

15 of 16 clip x qp cells reconstruct byte-identically to the decoder for EVERY
frame with the temporal field off. So the temporal MV field accounts for
essentially all of the remaining multi-frame defect, and everything else in the
video path -- prediction, residual, entropy, references, deblock, CDEF -- is
correct.

WHY IT HAS THIS SHAPE. Modes NEARESTMV and NEARMV do not signal an MV; they
DERIVE it from the reference-MV stack. Frame 1 is immune because its reference
is the key frame and the projection returns nothing for it (C aborts on
`start_frame_buf->frame_type == KEY_FRAME`, md_config_process.c:441), which is
why f0 and f1 were always clean. From the first frame whose reference is itself
an inter frame, the field is live; if the port's projection differs from the
decoder's, the two derive DIFFERENT MVs for the same block and the prediction
diverges wholesale -- which matches the observed signature (large luma deltas,
up to 34, over a whole block, spreading into neighbouring intra blocks that
predict from it).

This is exactly what the original inter-chain refusal named, so its diagnosis
was right; what was missing was any way to measure it.

ONE RESIDUAL, and it is NOT this defect: vidyo1 at qp20 still fails to decode
from f2 with MFMV off. That is a separate, narrower bug and it now has a clean
reproducer -- the only failing cell out of sixteen.

NEXT: compare the port's `setup_motion_field` / `motion_field_projection` output
against the decoder's derivation for one block of vidyo3 f3, which is the
smallest failing case (530 luma px in a 16-wide column, blocks mi=(36,44) and
(40,44), both mode 14 = NEARMV with mv=(-4,-52)).

================================================================
HYPOTHESES TESTED AND REFUTED while hunting the projection bug
================================================================

Recorded so the next session does not re-run them.

1. STALE MV GRID / intra blocks not resetting their cells. The writeback census
   reports `mfmv=1024/1024` on every inter frame -- every 8x8 cell naming a real
   reference -- which looks exactly like intra blocks failing to reset theirs,
   the thing that would make a later frame project motion through a block that
   has none. It is not: segmenting the packtree by its byte-offset resets shows
   the final pass of each inter frame on this cell commits 121/195/195/180
   blocks and ZERO of them are intra. `none=0` is therefore correct. The census
   now prints `none=` alongside `mfmv=` so this is one glance instead of an
   investigation.

   Watch the trap in the middle of that check: a naive census over the whole
   packtree counted 528 intra commits and 83,776 px of coded area against a
   65,536 px frame -- it was summing every MD pass, not the final decisions.
   Segment on the `off=` resets before drawing any per-frame conclusion.

2. C AS THE ORACLE FOR FRAME 2. Comparing the port's frame 2 against C's does
   not isolate this: on real video at 256x256 p6 the two already differ at f0
   (7389 vs 7374 B) and f1 (200 vs 234 B), which the real-video gate records as
   its own open surface. `fh_fields.py` on frame 2 names loop_filter_level as
   the first differing field, and that is a deblock SEARCH output -- downstream
   of a recon that already differs. The clean oracle is the DECODER, via
   encoder-recon-vs-decode self-consistency, not C.

3. NO C INTERPOSER IS AVAILABLE for the temporal field. `av1_setup_motion_field`
   and its projection helpers are `static` in md_config_process.c, so
   `-Wl,--wrap` cannot reach them; that is why capture_c_trace has no
   SVT_MFMV_OUT to join against. Getting C's tpl_mvs needs a source-level
   instrumentation build, not an interposer.

WHAT WOULD ACTUALLY LOCALISE IT. For a SKIP block (yeob=ueob=veob=0) the
decoder's reconstruction IS its prediction, so the MV the decoder derived can be
recovered by searching which MV reproduces that block from the byte-identical
reference. The failing blocks on vidyo3 f3 all carry residual, so pick a cell
that puts a skip block in the drifting region first -- or difference the two
reconstructions, since both sides decode the SAME residual and
`decoder_recon - port_recon` is exactly `decoder_pred - port_pred`.
